### A9 Copernicus conventions that affect every chain: spaces, signed values, normals (11 October 2026)

- Sources: https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/copernicus/spaces.html , https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/copernicus/normals.html , https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/copernicus/tips.html , https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/blur.html , https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/uvmapbyid.html (11 October 2026)
- SOURCE (spaces): Buffer holds the raw samples; Pixel is a pixel-sized area with its origin at the lower left; Texture runs 0 to 1 across the data window and stretches the image to fit; Image runs -1 to 1 across the display window and preserves the pixel aspect ratio, so non-square images leave borders; Local runs from minus half to plus half of the display window size in world units and also preserves aspect; World is a 3D modelling-space location; Copernicus favours image space because it is linear and can hold negative values, and values keep their natural range, so HDR and vector data are not clamped or normalized (11 October 2026)
- SOURCE (units on nodes): `blur` Units are Image (default), Pixels or Local; `uvmapbyid` and `sample` offer UV Space Texture (0 to 1) or Image (-1 to 1); `heighttoambientocclusion`, `heighttoshadow` and `slopedir` have a Space parameter; the pages read do not define the unit of `distort` Scale or `blur` Size in numbers, so the relation between a Designer intensity and a COP value must be probed (11 October 2026)
- UNVERIFIED reading: because Image space spans -1 to 1, a length of 1 in Image units is half of a square image width, so the small local `distort` Scale values (0.008 and 0.02, Copernicus1.md lines 1353 and 1398) would be fractions of that half-width; export a test image and measure the displacement in pixels before trusting this (11 October 2026)
- SOURCE (normals): Signed normals match Karma and SOP geometry (components can be negative, length 1) and Offset normals are the same vectors scaled into 0 to 1 (centered near 0.5 with length 0.5) as shaders usually expect for normal maps; the convention is chosen by Type Info on `layerproperties`, Conversion on `convertnormal` or Normal Type on `heighttonormal`; the page says nothing about OpenGL versus DirectX Y direction, so that must be checked on the node (LOCAL Copernicus1.md line 1573 notes that a DirectX normal needed flipY in a Principled Shader) (11 October 2026)
- SOURCE (tips): `copcache -c` in the textport clears GPU and memory usage; right-click Inspect in the Composite Viewer reads pixel values; the Performance Monitor with an OpenCL COP timing option gives GPU timings (11 October 2026)
- LOCAL: height, normal and roughness exports should use `rop_image` with colorconversion raw to avoid gamma (Copernicus1.md line 1521), and a measurement of a baked layer is safer through exported PNG files than through `layer.allBufferElements()` (line 1543) (11 October 2026)

